<Project>

  <!-- =============================================================================
       Local.targets.template — copy this to Local.targets and edit the paths.

       WHEN YOU WANT THIS: you are developing against Keri's source and need the
       freshly built assemblies in another local project before they are
       published. If you only consume released versions, install the packages
       from nuget.org instead — this file solves a different problem.

       SETUP:
         1. Copy this file to Local.targets in the same folder (Keri.Epicor/).
         2. Edit the KeriCopyDest paths below to point at your consumer project
            lib/ folders.
         3. Build the solution in Release — the DLLs copy automatically.

       Local.targets is gitignored, so each developer keeps their own list of
       destinations without committing local paths to the repo. Keri.Epicor.csproj
       imports it conditionally:

           <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Local.targets"
                   Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Local.targets')" />

       The Exists() condition means a build without this file behaves normally.

       WHAT GETS COPIED:
       Every *.dll from three output folders — this project's, plus Keri.Files'
       and Keri.Mail's. That yields the four Keri assemblies and whatever NuGet
       dependencies each project's build placed beside them (Newtonsoft.Json,
       ClosedXML and its graph, MailKit/MimeKit and theirs). The *.dll wildcard
       filters out .pdb, .xml, .deps.json and .dll.config.

       Drop the Keri.Files or Keri.Mail line if your consumer does not use them.

       CHECK THE COUNT. The Message below reports how many DLLs were copied. If
       that number is smaller than you expect, a source path is not resolving —
       an unbuilt project, or a folder renamed since this file was written. A
       glob that matches nothing expands to nothing silently, so the count is
       the only signal you get. (This template previously named projects from
       before the Keri rename and quietly copied three DLLs while describing
       nineteen.)

       Only Release / net8.0 triggers the copy. Debug and net48 builds are
       silent; change the condition below if your consumers target net48.

       FILE LIFECYCLE: the copy is additive. Removing a dependency from Keri does
       not delete its DLL from a consumer's lib/ folder — clean those up by hand.

       For a full refresh, build everything rather than one project:
           dotnet build KineticRESTIntegrator.sln -c Release
       ============================================================================= -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Each KeriCopyDest is a destination folder. Add as many as you need. -->
    <KeriCopyDest Include="C:\path\to\consumer1\lib" />
    <KeriCopyDest Include="C:\path\to\consumer2\lib" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="CopyKeriDllsToConsumers"
          AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release' AND '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0'">

    <ItemGroup>
      <!-- This project's output: Keri.Epicor, Keri.RestTransport, and their deps. -->
      <KeriDll Include="$(TargetDir)*.dll" />

      <!-- The sibling packages. Each resolves only if that project has been
           built in Release/net8.0; see CHECK THE COUNT above. -->
      <KeriDll Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\Keri.Files\bin\Release\net8.0\*.dll" />
      <KeriDll Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\Keri.Mail\bin\Release\net8.0\*.dll" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Copy SourceFiles="@(KeriDll)"
          DestinationFolder="%(KeriCopyDest.Identity)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />

    <Message Importance="high"
             Text="Copied @(KeriDll->Count()) DLLs to %(KeriCopyDest.Identity)" />

  </Target>

</Project>
